hat red message is a warning, not necessarily a configuration failure:

“The metadata file specifies multiple bindings for the login URL.”

It means the CyberArk IdP metadata you imported advertises more than one SAML SSO binding, typically HTTP-POST and HTTP-Redirect, while Salesforce wants you to select which binding to use for SP-initiated authentication. Salesforce explicitly supports either HTTP POST or HTTP Redirect for that setting.

For this POC, I would do the following:

Leave Service Provider Initiated Request Binding = HTTP Redirect if that is the binding configured for the CyberArk SAML app.
Keep the CyberArk Identity Provider Login URL that was populated from the metadata.
The warning itself can remain; it is simply telling you that the metadata contained multiple possible bindings.
Make sure you upload the CyberArk Identity Provider signing certificate under Identity Provider Certificate before saving/testing.

One other important correction from your screenshot: you currently have:

SAML Identity Type:
● Assertion contains the User's Salesforce username

For the mapping we discussed, change this to:

SAML Identity Type:
● Assertion contains the Federation ID from the User object

And keep:

SAML Identity Location:
● Identity is in the NameIdentifier element
  of the Subject statement

So the intended configuration is:

SAML Identity Type:
Federation ID

SAML Identity Location:
NameIdentifier / Subject

Service Provider Initiated Request Binding:
HTTP Redirect

Identity Provider Login URL:
CyberArk app login URL

Identity Provider Certificate:
CyberArk IdP signing certificate

The multiple bindings warning is therefore not the item I would troubleshoot first. The two things I would correct before proceeding are Federation ID selection and the missing IdP signing certificate visible in the screenshot.
